                "type": "text",
                "text": "Count 3 ways to swap in two pairs and 6 ways to move in one cycle of four.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Either the students swap in two pairs or all four move in one cycle.\n\nFor two pairs, the first student has 3 possible partners; the remaining two must swap. This gives 3 ways.\n\nFor one cycle of four, fix the first student and order the other three around the cycle. This gives $3!=6$ ways.\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=3+3!\\\\N&=3+6\\\\N&=9\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
